Thank you for the thoughtful reply. Your emphasis on boundaries, relations, and the construction of experience deserves serious attention. From an EFMW research perspective, I would ask where the proposed architecture is defined, what follows from it, and what observations could distinguish it from alternatives. I would hold EFMW to that same standard.

First, a constructed experience is not necessarily an unreal experience. Showing that a system constructs a representation of redness does not, by itself, show that the experienced quality does not exist. A relational property can still be real. The question is what you mean by “qualia”: are you rejecting intrinsically private, ineffable properties, or qualitative experience altogether? Those are different claims.

Second, the Vanished Present offers an interesting account of access: nervous systems receive delayed information and integrate it into a usable present. But delayed access does not establish that the present itself is absent or that the world flickers. A sensor can sample a continuous process intermittently. To move from a model of observation to an ontology, we need an additional argument or a discriminating prediction.

Third, connecting prediction error, emotion, action, and self-maintenance is a worthwhile program, but compatibility with predictive processing is not yet subsumption. To demonstrate the stronger claim, specify the correspondence between variables and update rules, show which results are recovered, and identify a prediction your architecture makes that an appropriate alternative does not. Prior work already connects prediction and affect, as well as action and agency: see Joffily and Coricelli on emotional valence and Friston and colleagues on action. Neither paper resolves phenomenality merely by modeling a function.

Your idea that feeling compresses information about threats to important values is a possible functional account of affect. But “necessary,” “fastest,” or “most efficient” requires a defined task and comparisons with alternative mechanisms. And we should distinguish the information a signal carries, the behavior it influences, and the question of why it is experienced as felt. A model of the first two does not automatically settle the third.

The point I would revise most strongly is the claim that a theory derived from axioms proves those axioms in turn. A coherent derivation shows what follows if the premises hold. Successful predictions can supply evidence for a framework, but different premises may yield the same observations. The theory cannot certify its own starting assumptions simply by being internally consistent.

A constructive next step would be to isolate your claim about protecting a self-narrative. Define the narrative state, the relevant prediction error, and the rule for updating beliefs. Then specify, before examining the test data, a situation where your model and a named comparison model predict different outcomes. A successful test would support that bounded architectural claim. A failure would tell us what needs revision. Neither result alone would prove or eliminate phenomenal experience.

What I see here is a potentially fruitful research proposal connecting delayed perception, constructed continuity, valuation, and self-maintenance. Its stronger conclusions remain open. The best way forward is not to make either framework win by definition, but to find the point at which their commitments produce different, inspectable consequences.
